The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
int err = do_sigpending(&set, sizeof(old_sigset_t)); 
if (err == 0)
    if (copy_to_user(set32, &set, sizeof(old_sigset_t)))
        err = -EFAULT;
```
Reason:
The `do_sigpending` function is called with `sizeof(old_sigset_t)` (8 bytes), which is larger than the expected size of `compat_old_sigset_t` (4 bytes). This results in writing more bytes than the user space can handle. Additionally, on big-endian architectures, the order of the bytes written is incorrect, leading to further misrepresentation of the signal set.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
int err = do_sigpending(&set, sizeof(set.sig[0]));
if (!err)
    err = put_user(set.sig[0], set32);
```
Reason:
The patch changes the size passed to `do_sigpending` to `sizeof(set.sig[0])`, which correctly matches the expected size of 4 bytes for `compat_old_sigset_t`. It also replaces `copy_to_user` with `put_user`, which is more appropriate for writing a single value, ensuring that the correct data is written to user space without exceeding the expected size.

